TN='Activities and experiments'

L(2,TN,'Cells: hands-on activities from the textbook','See the cell for yourself',
 "A cell is too small to see with the eye, so scientists measure and observe it with a microscope. Chapter 2 gives five activities that let you measure a cell, watch water move through one and compare plant and animal cells.",
 [("Activity 2.1 estimates the size of a cell. First place a transparent millimetre ruler on the microscope stage and measure the diameter of the field of view. A 5 mm diameter is 5 x 1000 = 5000 micrometres.","measure field of view"),
  ("Then replace the ruler with an onion peel, focus, and count how many cells lie along the diameter. Cell size is the field diameter divided by the number of cells.","size = diameter / number"),
  ("Activity 2.2 uses two equal pieces of potato. Weigh both, put one in plain water and the other in a 20 per cent salt or sugar solution, and leave them for about an hour. Weigh again and compare.","potato experiment"),
  ("The piece in plain water gains weight because water enters its cells, and the piece in the concentrated solution loses weight because water leaves. This movement of water through the cell membrane is osmosis.","osmosis"),
  ("Activity 2.3 compares cells. Make a temporary slide of an onion or Rhoeo leaf peel stained with safranin, and a slide of cheek cells stained with methylene blue.","two slides"),
  ("Onion or Rhoeo cells are box-shaped and regularly arranged because of the cell wall, whereas cheek cells are irregularly arranged. Adding 20 per cent sugar solution makes plant cell contents shrink.","plant and animal cells"),
  ("Activity 2.4 asks you to study labelled diagrams of a bacterial cell, a plant cell and an animal cell and compare organelles such as the nucleus, chloroplast, mitochondria and cell wall.","compare diagrams"),
  ("Activity 2.5 grows onion roots in water for 5 to 6 days. Root tips are preserved in aceto-alcohol, softened with dilute hydrochloric acid and stained with aceto-carmine to see dividing cells.","onion root tip")],
 ("In Activity 2.1 the field of view is 5 mm across and 25 onion cells fit along the diameter. Estimate one cell.",[("Convert the field diameter to micrometres: 5 x 1000 = 5000 micrometres.","1 mm = 1000 micrometres."),("Divide by the number of cells: 5000 / 25.","Size = diameter / number of cells."),("One onion cell is about 200 micrometres long.","Within the typical range seen in the textbook.")]),
 ("Describe two activities from this chapter and what each shows.",["Potato in water and salt solution shows osmosis","Onion and cheek cells show plant and animal differences","Cell size is field diameter divided by number of cells"],["Thinking the potato changes weight for no reason"]),
 ["Cell size can be estimated from the field of view and a cell count.","A potato gains weight in plain water and loses weight in strong salt or sugar solution because of osmosis.","Plant cells are box-shaped with a cell wall; cheek cells are irregular.","Onion root tips show dividing cells when stained."],
 [num("l2-1",1,"A field of view 5 mm across is how many micrometres?",5000,"1 mm = 1000 micrometres, so 5 mm = 5000 micrometres.",[(5,"That is the value in millimetres.")],"micrometres"),
  mcq("l2-2",1,"A potato piece kept in plain water for an hour","gains weight",["loses weight","stays dry"],"Water enters the cells by osmosis."),
  mcq("l2-3",1,"A potato piece kept in 20 per cent salt solution","loses weight",["gains weight","turns green"],"Water leaves the cells."),
  tf("l2-4",1,"Onion peel cells and cheek cells look the same under the microscope.",False,"Onion cells are box-shaped and regularly arranged; cheek cells are irregular."),
  mcq("l2-5",2,"Cheek cells are stained with","methylene blue",["safranin","iodine only"],"The activity uses methylene blue for cheek cells."),
  mcq("l2-6",2,"Cell size is estimated by","dividing the field diameter by the number of cells",["multiplying them","adding them"],"Size = diameter / number of cells."),
  mcq("l2-7",2,"The stain used on onion root tips in Activity 2.5 is","aceto-carmine",["methylene blue","safranin only"],"It stains dividing cells."),
  short("l2-8",3,"What is the movement of water through the cell membrane called?",["osmosis"],"Osmosis moves water across the membrane.")])

L(3,TN,'Tissues: hands-on activities from the textbook','Growth, skin and movement',
 "Why does a cut onion root stop growing, why does a cut ooze blood, and why can your elbow bend but your skull cannot? Chapter 3 answers with simple activities.",
 [("Activity 3.1 designs an experiment with two onion bulbs in jars of water. Measure the roots on days 1, 2 and 3.","two jars"),
  ("On day 3, cut about 1 cm off the root tips of the bulb in Jar B and measure both again for four more days.","cut the tips"),
  ("The roots with cut tips stop growing while the others continue. Growth is concentrated at the root tip, which has dividing meristematic tissue.","growth at the tip"),
  ("Activity 3.2 links everyday experience to tissue. A small cut oozes red blood and then a clot forms, so blood is a tissue that carries substances and seals wounds.","blood clots"),
  ("Activity 3.3 identifies connective tissues by action. Touching your elbow feels hard and rigid and gives strength and support, which is bone tissue.","bone tissue"),
  ("Activity 3.4 asks what fraction of body weight comes from bones and muscles. On average adult males have about 40 to 50 per cent muscle and females about 30 to 40 per cent, while bone is about 12 to 15 per cent for all adults.","bone and muscle share"),
  ("Activity 3.5 asks you to move body parts and record what each can do: complete rotation, partial rotation, or only bending. Joints decide the kind of movement.","joint movements"),
  ("Together the activities show that tissues are groups of similar cells doing a particular job.","tissue is a job team")],
 ("A student weighs 50 kg. Using the textbook average of 40 per cent muscle for an adult male, estimate muscle mass. (Illustration only.)",[("Muscle share = 40 per cent of body weight.","Use the stated average."),("40 / 100 x 50 = 20.","Percentage of the weight."),("Muscle mass is about 20 kg for this illustration.","Estimate, not a measurement.")]),
 ("What does the onion root experiment show and how?",["Cut tips stop growing","Growth is at the root tip","Meristematic tissue divides"],["Thinking all parts of a root grow equally"]),
 ["Root growth is at the tip where dividing tissue is found.","Blood forms a clot to seal a cut.","Bone gives rigid support; muscles and joints allow movement.","Adult bone is about 12 to 15 per cent of body weight."],
 [mcq("l3-1",1,"After the root tips of one onion bulb are cut, its roots","stop growing",["grow faster","turn red"],"Growth happens at the tip."),
  tf("l3-2",1,"A cut that oozes blood later forms a clot.",True,"The clot seals the wound."),
  mcq("l3-3",1,"The hard rigid structure felt at the elbow is","bone",["fat","skin"],"Bone is a connective tissue that gives support."),
  mcq("l3-4",1,"Bone is about what share of adult body weight?","12 to 15 per cent",["50 per cent","1 per cent"],"The textbook gives about 12 to 15 per cent."),
  mcq("l3-5",2,"Dividing cells that cause root growth form","meristematic tissue",["muscle tissue","nervous tissue"],"The root tip has meristematic tissue."),
  tf("l3-6",2,"Adult males have about 40 to 50 per cent muscle on average.",True,"That is the average given in the activity."),
  mcq("l3-7",2,"The kind of movement a body part makes depends on its","joint",["colour","hair"],"Joints decide rotation or bending."),
  short("l3-8",3,"Which tissue seals a cut by forming a clot?",["blood"],"Blood forms a clot.")])

L(4,TN,'Motion: hands-on activities from the textbook','Throw, plot and measure',
 "Motion becomes clear when you throw a ball, plot a graph and spin a marble. Chapter 4 turns these into activities.",
 [("Activity 4.1 throws a ball straight up from O to a highest point B and back to O. Distance is the total path, so it adds the up and down paths, while displacement is the straight change of position and is zero when the ball returns to O.","distance vs displacement"),
  ("Activity 4.2 finds average acceleration. Look up the time a car takes to go from 0 to 100 km per hour and divide the change in speed by that time.","average acceleration"),
  ("For example 100 km per hour is about 27.8 m per second, so a car taking 10 seconds has an acceleration of about 2.8 m per second squared.","a = change in speed / time"),
  ("Activity 4.3 teaches graph plotting. Draw the X-axis and Y-axis on graph paper, decide which quantity goes on which axis, mark points from the table and join them.","plot a graph"),
  ("Activity 4.4 shows the slope of a position-time graph. A triangle drawn on a straight section gives change in position divided by change in time, which is the velocity.","slope is velocity"),
  ("A car moving at a steady 72 km per hour, which is 20 m per second, has a horizontal velocity-time graph. A car starting from rest whose velocity increases steadily gives a slanted straight line.","velocity-time graphs"),
  ("Activity 4.5 sends a marble round the inside of a ring. If you lift the ring, the marble leaves the circle and moves in a straight line.","circular motion"),
  ("This shows that the direction of motion keeps changing in circular motion, so it is accelerated motion even at steady speed.","direction changes")],
 ("A car goes from 0 to 27.8 m per second in 10 seconds. Find its average acceleration.",[("Change in speed = 27.8 - 0 = 27.8 m per second.","Final minus initial speed."),("Time taken = 10 s.","From the data."),("Acceleration = 27.8 / 10 = 2.78 m per second squared.","a = change in speed / time.")]),
 ("Explain the difference between distance and displacement with the ball-throwing activity.",["Distance is the total path travelled","Displacement is change of position","Displacement is zero at the start point"],["Thinking they are always equal"]),
 ["Distance is path length; displacement is the straight change of position.","Acceleration = change in speed divided by time.","Slope of a position-time graph gives velocity.","A marble leaving a ring moves in a straight line."],
 [mcq("l4-1",1,"A ball thrown straight up returns to the thrower's hand. Its displacement is","zero",["the total path","twice the height"],"The start and end positions are the same."),
  tf("l4-2",1,"Distance and displacement are always equal.",False,"They differ when the path is not a straight line or the object returns."),
  num("l4-3",1,"A car speeds up from 0 to 20 m per second in 5 s. Acceleration in m per second squared?",4,"20 / 5 = 4.",[(100,"That multiplies instead of dividing.")],"m per second squared"),
  mcq("l4-4",1,"The slope of a position-time graph gives","velocity",["acceleration","mass"],"Change in position divided by change in time is velocity."),
  mcq("l4-5",2,"A velocity-time graph of constant velocity is","a horizontal line",["a vertical line","a curve"],"Velocity does not change."),
  mcq("l4-6",2,"When the ring is lifted the marble moves","in a straight line",["in a circle","backwards"],"With no ring to bend its path it goes straight."),
  tf("l4-7",2,"72 km per hour is 20 m per second.",True,"72 x 1000 / 3600 = 20."),
  short("l4-8",3,"What is plotted on the X-axis of a position-time graph?",["time"],"Time is on the X-axis.")])

L(5,TN,'Mixtures: hands-on activities from the textbook','Solutions, suspensions and separation',
 "Salt vanishes in water, chalk does not, and oil floats on top. Chapter 5 lets you test these differences and separate mixtures yourself.",
 [("Activity 5.1 prepares three mixtures in beakers, such as salt in water, chalk powder in water and a third type, and passes a thin laser beam through each.","three mixtures"),
  ("In the salt solution the beam is not visible, but in the chalk suspension and in the colloid the particles scatter the light so the path shows. That is the Tyndall effect.","Tyndall effect"),
  ("Activity 5.2 plots a solubility curve, solubility in grams against temperature, for two solutes A and B in water.","solubility curve"),
  ("Activity 5.3 prepares crystals, for example of copper sulfate or common salt, by cooling a saturated solution. Copper sulfate is toxic, so work under supervision.","crystallisation, safety first"),
  ("Activity 5.5 does paper chromatography. Mark a spot of black sketch pen 2 cm from the bottom of the strip and dip the strip's end in water, and the colours of the ink separate.","chromatography"),
  ("Activity 5.6 pours mustard oil and water into a separating funnel. Two layers form and the oil, being lighter, floats on top and can be run off.","separating funnel"),
  ("Activity 5.7 heats camphor mixed with sand under an inverted funnel with a cotton plug. Camphor sublimes and its vapour collects on the funnel as solid camphor while the sand stays behind.","sublimation"),
  ("Activity 5.8 builds a model centrifuge from a cardboard disc and thread to see that heavier particles move outward when spun. Activity 5.9 compares solutions, suspensions and colloids in a table.","centrifuge and comparison table")],
 ("Which method separates mustard oil from water, and which separates camphor from sand?",[("Oil and water do not mix and form two layers, so use a separating funnel.","Immiscible liquids."),("Camphor sublimes on heating but sand does not, so use sublimation.","One component sublimes."),("Each method uses a different physical property.","Match method to property.")]),
 ("Explain how the laser beam shows the difference between a solution and a suspension.",["Beam invisible in solution","Beam scattered by particles in suspension and colloid","Called the Tyndall effect"],["Thinking all mixtures scatter light"]),
 ["Solutions do not scatter a light beam; suspensions and colloids do.","Chromatography separates coloured substances in ink.","Immiscible liquids separate with a separating funnel.","A substance that sublimes can be separated from sand by heating."],
 [mcq("l5-1",1,"In the laser activity the beam path was NOT visible in","the salt solution",["the chalk suspension","the colloid"],"Solution particles are too small to scatter light."),
  tf("l5-2",1,"Mustard oil floats on water in a separating funnel.",True,"Oil is lighter than water."),
  mcq("l5-3",1,"Camphor and sand can be separated by","sublimation",["filtration","centrifuging"],"Camphor vapourises on heating without melting."),
  mcq("l5-4",1,"In chromatography the pen spot is made about","2 cm from the bottom of the strip",["at the top","in the water"],"The spot must stay above the water level."),
  mcq("l5-5",2,"Scattering of light by particles is called the","Tyndall effect",["Doppler effect","greenhouse effect"],"It makes a beam visible in a colloid."),
  mcq("l5-6",2,"A graph of solubility against temperature is a","solubility curve",["velocity graph","pie chart"],"Activity 5.2 plots it."),
  tf("l5-7",2,"Copper sulfate is safe to touch with bare hands.",False,"It is toxic; work under supervision."),
  short("l5-8",3,"Which liquid floats on water in Activity 5.6?",["mustard oil","oil"],"Mustard oil forms the upper layer.")])

L(6,TN,'Forces: hands-on activities from the textbook','Friction, inertia and reaction',
 "Why do coins slide farther on a polished floor, and why does a chair move back when you push a table? Chapter 6's activities answer these.",
 [("Activity 6.1 stacks four coins and pushes them on level surfaces of different materials. The stack travels farthest on the smoothest surface.","coins on surfaces"),
  ("Activity 6.2 pulls a wooden block with a spring balance. The reading when it just moves is an approximate measure of the force of friction, smallest on the surface where the coins went farthest.","spring balance and friction"),
  ("If there were no friction a moving object would keep moving. This tendency to resist change in motion is inertia.","inertia"),
  ("Activities 6.3 and 6.4 are teacher demonstrations with a cart pulled by a cup of weights. Doubling the mass of the cart while the force stays the same gives a smaller acceleration.","force, mass, acceleration"),
  ("Activity 6.5 has you sit on a wheeled chair with legs raised and push a heavy table. You move backward as the table is pushed forward, showing equal and opposite reaction.","action and reaction"),
  ("Activity 6.6 joins two identical spring balances by their hooks and pulls. Both read the same, showing action and reaction forces are equal in magnitude.","equal readings"),
  ("Activity 6.7 sends an inflated balloon along a thread with a straw. The air rushes backward and the balloon moves forward.","balloon rocket"),
  ("Together these activities support Newton's three laws of motion.","three laws")],
 ("Why does the balloon move forward when the air escapes backward?",[("The balloon pushes air backward.","Action."),("The air pushes the balloon forward with an equal and opposite force.","Reaction."),("So the balloon moves forward, which is how rockets work.","Third law.")]),
 ("Describe how you could show Newton's third law at home.",["Chair and table push","Two spring balances read equal","Balloon rocket"],["Thinking the reaction acts on the same object"]),
 ["Friction is smaller on smooth surfaces, so objects travel farther.","Inertia is the tendency to resist change in motion.","For the same force a heavier cart accelerates less.","Action and reaction are equal and opposite on different objects."],
 [mcq("l6-1",1,"A stack of coins goes farthest on","the smoothest surface",["the roughest surface","a carpet"],"Less friction on smooth surfaces."),
  mcq("l6-2",1,"The spring balance reading when the block just moves measures approximately","the force of friction",["the weight of the block only","the speed"],"It balances friction."),
  tf("l6-3",1,"The two spring balances in Activity 6.6 read different values.",False,"Their readings are equal."),
  mcq("l6-4",1,"The tendency of objects to resist a change in motion is","inertia",["friction","gravity"],"Newton described it as inertia."),
  mcq("l6-5",2,"A balloon rocket moves forward because air is pushed","backward",["upward","sideways"],"The reaction pushes the balloon forward."),
  mcq("l6-6",2,"Doubling the mass of the cart with the same force makes its acceleration","smaller",["greater","unchanged"],"a = F/m."),
  tf("l6-7",2,"Action and reaction forces act on the same object.",False,"They act on different objects."),
  short("l6-8",3,"Which law explains the balloon rocket?",["third law","newton's third law","third"],"Newton's third law of motion.")])

L(7,TN,'Work, energy and machines: hands-on activities from the textbook','Dropping balls and balancing beams',
 "Energy changes form as a pendulum swings, and a small effort can lift a heavy load with a lever. Chapter 7's activities show it.",
 [("Activity 7.1 drops a heavy ball into loose sand from 1 m and then 2 m. The higher drop makes a deeper depression because the ball has more energy.","ball in sand"),
  ("Activity 7.2 uses a pendulum with a white sheet behind it. At the extreme positions it has only potential energy, and at the lowest point it has maximum kinetic energy.","energy in a pendulum"),
  ("Activity 7.3 pulls a cart up a plank with a spring balance and also lifts it vertically. The reading is smaller on the slope, so an inclined plane makes the effort smaller.","inclined plane"),
  ("Activity 7.4 balances a stapler and an eraser on a scale over a pencil. This is a lever, and the position of the pivot decides how much effort is needed.","a lever"),
  ("Activity 7.5 hangs a scale from its midpoint with a string as a beam balance and uses identical coins as weights.","beam balance"),
  ("Equal weights at equal distances from the fulcrum balance the beam.","balance condition"),
  ("A machine does not create energy. It lets a smaller force act over a longer distance or changes the direction of force.","machines"),
  ("Work is force times distance moved in the direction of the force.","work formula")],
 ("A force of 20 N moves a box 3 m in its own direction. How much work is done?",[("Work = force x distance.","Definition."),("20 x 3 = 60.","Multiply."),("Work done is 60 joules.","Unit of work is the joule.")]),
 ("How does a pendulum show the conversion of energy?",["Potential energy highest at the ends","Kinetic energy highest at the lowest point","Total energy stays nearly constant"],["Thinking energy is lost completely"]),
 ["A higher drop gives more energy and a deeper dent.","A pendulum changes potential energy to kinetic energy and back.","An inclined plane reduces the effort needed.","A lever works on the pivot position."],
 [mcq("l7-1",1,"A ball dropped from 2 m makes a depression that is","deeper than one from 1 m",["shallower","the same"],"It has more energy."),
  mcq("l7-2",1,"At the lowest point a swinging pendulum has maximum","kinetic energy",["potential energy","mass"],"Speed is greatest there."),
  num("l7-3",1,"Work done by 20 N through 3 m in joules?",60,"20 x 3 = 60.",[(23,"That adds instead of multiplying.")],"joules"),
  tf("l7-4",1,"A machine creates new energy.",False,"It only makes work easier."),
  mcq("l7-5",2,"A scale resting on a pencil with a stapler at one end is a","lever",["pulley","wheel"],"The pencil is the fulcrum."),
  mcq("l7-6",2,"On an inclined plane the spring balance reads","less than lifting straight up",["more","the same"],"The effort is smaller over a longer path."),
  tf("l7-7",2,"Equal weights at equal distances balance a beam.",True,"That is the balance condition."),
  short("l7-8",3,"What is the SI unit of work?",["joule","joules","j"],"Work is measured in joules.")])

L(10,TN,'Sound: hands-on activities from the textbook','Hear the vibration',
 "Sound is a vibration you can feel. Chapter 10's activities let you pluck, tap and spin to see how it travels.",
 [("Activity 10.1 stretches a rubber band across an open box and plucks it. You hear a sound while it vibrates.","plucked rubber band"),
  ("Activity 10.2 strikes a tuning fork on a rubber pad. You hear the sound in any direction, so it travels in all directions from the source.","tuning fork"),
  ("Activity 10.3 compares hearing a knock on a desk through air and through the desk with your ear against it. Sound also travels through solids.","sound in solids"),
  ("Activity 10.4 taps two spoons under water and still hears the sound, so liquids carry sound too.","sound in liquids"),
  ("Activity 10.5 uses a slinky with a mark on one turn. A push makes compressions and rarefactions travel along while the marked turn only moves back and forth, so sound is a longitudinal wave.","slinky waves"),
  ("Activity 10.6 stretches a cellophane sheet over a container and sounds a metal plate near it. Grains on the sheet jump because the sheet vibrates.","grains jumping"),
  ("Activities 10.7 and 10.8 use a sound app. Raising the frequency makes the pitch higher, and a thin rubber band vibrates faster than a thick one.","pitch and frequency"),
  ("So frequency decides pitch, and a higher frequency means a shorter time period.","f and T")],
 ("Why does a thin rubber band give a higher pitch than a thick one?",[("The thin band vibrates faster.","Higher frequency."),("Higher frequency means a higher pitch.","Pitch rises with frequency."),("Its time period is shorter.","T = 1/f.")]),
 ("What do the slinky and the cellophane activities show about sound?",["Sound is a longitudinal wave of compressions","The medium vibrates but does not travel","Vibrating sheets move grains"],["Thinking sound travels in a vacuum"]),
 ["Sound is produced by vibrations.","It travels through solids, liquids and gases.","Sound is a longitudinal wave of compressions and rarefactions.","Higher frequency gives higher pitch and a shorter time period."],
 [tf("l10-1",1,"A plucked rubber band vibrates and makes a sound.",True,"Vibration produces sound."),
  mcq("l10-2",1,"Sound is heard in water in Activity 10.4, so water","carries sound",["blocks sound","absorbs all sound"],"Liquids are media for sound."),
  mcq("l10-3",1,"A slinky shows sound as a","longitudinal wave",["transverse wave only","light wave"],"Compressions and rarefactions move along the coil."),
  mcq("l10-4",1,"A thin rubber band vibrates","faster than a thick one",["slower than a thick one","not at all"],"Higher frequency, higher pitch."),
  mcq("l10-5",2,"Higher frequency gives","a higher pitch",["a lower pitch","a louder sound only"],"Pitch depends on frequency."),
  tf("l10-6",2,"Sound travels better through a desk than you hear it through air in Activity 10.3.",True,"Solids carry sound well."),
  mcq("l10-7",2,"Grains jump on the cellophane sheet because the sheet","vibrates",["melts","cools"],"The sound makes the sheet vibrate."),
  short("l10-8",3,"What type of wave is sound?",["longitudinal","longitudinal wave"],"Sound is a longitudinal wave.")])

L(11,TN,'Reproduction: hands-on activities from the textbook','Budding, moulds and flowers',
 "Plants make new plants from cuttings, yeast makes buds and flowers carry the parts of reproduction. Chapter 11 invites you to see each.",
 [("Activity 11.1 asks you to talk to gardeners and observe cutting, grafting and layering, three ways of vegetative propagation.","cutting, grafting, layering"),
  ("Activity 11.2 mixes yeast in sugar solution, keeps it warm for one or two hours and looks at a drop under a microscope, where buds on yeast cells show budding.","yeast budding"),
  ("Activity 11.3 keeps a moist slice of bread or roti in a damp chamber to grow bread mould, showing reproduction by spores.","bread mould"),
  ("Activity 11.4 uses coloured beads for chromosomes to model how crossing over mixes characters, which explains variation.","bead model of variation"),
  ("Activity 11.5 collects flowers and records the floral parts from the outer whorl to the inner one in a table.","flower parts"),
  ("Activity 11.6 uses pea flowers. Remove the stamens from some buds and flowers, then compare seed formation, which shows the role of pollination.","pea flowers"),
  ("Pollination is the transfer of pollen to the stigma, within the same flower or between flowers of the same plant (self-pollination) or of different plants (cross-pollination).","pollination"),
  ("Activity 11.7 compares pollen production and seed formation for different pollination strategies in a table.","pollen data")],
 ("Name the three methods of vegetative propagation in Activity 11.1.",[("Cutting plants a piece of stem.","Method one."),("Grafting joins a shoot to a rooted plant.","Method two."),("Layering roots a bent branch while it is still attached.","Method three.")]),
 ("What do the yeast and bread mould activities show?",["Yeast reproduces by budding","Bread mould reproduces by spores","Both are microscopic life"],["Thinking they need seeds"]),
 ["Cutting, grafting and layering are vegetative propagation.","Yeast reproduces by budding.","Bread mould reproduces by spores.","Pollination is the transfer of pollen to the stigma."],
 [mcq("l11-1",1,"Yeast in warm sugar solution shows reproduction by","budding",["seeds","fragmentation"],"Yeast cells form buds."),
  mcq("l11-2",1,"Bread mould spreads by","spores",["seeds","buds only"],"Spores germinate on moist bread."),
  tf("l11-3",1,"Cutting, grafting and layering are ways of vegetative propagation.",True,"They use plant parts, not seeds."),
  mcq("l11-4",1,"Transfer of pollen to the stigma is","pollination",["fertilisation","germination"],"Pollination comes before fertilisation."),
  mcq("l11-5",2,"Coloured beads on chromatids model","how crossing over mixes characters",["flower colours only","seed size"],"It shows variation."),
  tf("l11-6",2,"Self-pollination means pollen reaches a stigma of the same plant.",True,"Same flower or same plant."),
  mcq("l11-7",2,"In Activity 11.6 the stamens are removed to","control pollination",["colour the petals","grow roots"],"Without stamens no self-pollen is produced."),
  short("l11-8",3,"Name one method of vegetative propagation.",["cutting","grafting","layering"],"Cutting, grafting and layering qualify.")])
print('done')
